{"kind":"assert","text":"The first of these three surfaces is confirmed in the code and measured in the live log, and the measurement changes what the work should cover.\n\nConfirmed in the code. foldState.staleness in internal/workroom/fold.go skips any record whose decision verdict is not Effective before it looks at that record's bases. An ineffective record therefore never gets an entry in the stale map. So for any effective record resting on an ineffective basis, the staleBasis test is false by construction, whatever happens underneath that hop. The request describes this accurately.\n\nMeasured in the live log, which the request did not do. 39 pairs exist today where an effective record rests on a basis that is not effective. Twelve of those dependents are artifacts, which is exactly the harm the request names: an artifact that will project current for ever because the hop beneath it cannot carry staleness.\n\nThe larger half of the population is not what the request describes, and this is the part worth acting on. Of the 39, twenty-one rest on a basis that has no decision and is not a statement either — it resolves to nothing at all. Seventeen unique bases, and their shapes say what happened: six are canonical forty-plus-forty identifiers that simply name no record, five are event ids truncated part-way through the second half, three are commit identifiers missing the event half entirely, two are bare hex with no scheme, and one is something else again.\n\nSo the policy question the request poses — propagate through the ineffective hop, or flag resting-on-ineffective at statement time — is posed over the smaller case. An ineffective basis is a record that exists and was refused; a dangling basis is a citation to something that never existed. They want different answers, and only the second can be checked at statement time without waiting for a verdict.\n\nWhy it stays invisible, which I can speak to from the wrong side. validateBasis walks only the citing kind's declared basis constraints, and a reference resolving to no record is skipped with a continue; a kind declaring no basis constraints never enters that loop at all. That is why a fabricated identifier lands as an effective statement. I produced eleven fabricated or truncated identifiers in this repository, and the fold accepted every one whose kind carried no basis constraint. Six canonical-but-absent bases in the live log are that failure, made durable.\n\nI am not proposing the remedy here, for the same reason the request does not: whether a dangling citation should be refused at admission, flagged in the projection, or reported by a separate check is a judgement about where the discipline should bite, and it interacts with the stated design that gitseq has no ontology. But whoever takes this should know the population is 39, that twelve dependents are artifacts, and that most of it is dangling rather than ineffective."}